<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  OrchiconSetup.wxs - the Windows SETUP EXPERIENCE: one wizard, real progress.

  WHY THIS EXISTS. The MSI is the right unit of deployment (Intune, SCCM, Group Policy and
  `msiexec /qn` all speak it) but a bare MSI is a POOR EXPERIENCE for a person: it draws the
  wizard, then hands the interesting part — a multi-hundred-megabyte download with prompts —
  to a console window it cannot see or report on. The operator asked, reasonably, whether the
  whole thing could live in the wizard instead. A Burn bundle is the Windows answer to that:
  it is a bootstrapper that OWNS the UI and CHAINS packages, so it can show real progress per
  package, expose the log, and hand over cleanly.

  WHAT IT IS NOT: a replacement for the MSI. The bundle wraps it. An estate that deploys with
  `msiexec /qn` keeps working exactly as before, and this .exe is for people.

  THE DIVISION OF LABOUR, and it is deliberate:
    * the BUNDLE owns the wizard, the licence page, progress and the log;
    * the MSI owns installation of the files (per-user, no elevation);
    * `orchicon install` still owns the plane itself — it is the same code path the one-line
      PowerShell install runs, and duplicating it here is how the two start disagreeing about
      what "installed" means.

  THE SETUP STEP STAYS INSIDE THE MSI (its custom action), NOT HERE, and the reason is
  concrete rather than stylistic: `orchicon install` calls os.Executable() to find the sibling
  `orch` binary and to launch the runtime daemon. A Burn ExePackage runs a cached COPY from
  its own folder, so the daemon would be registered against a temp path that later disappears.
  The install must run the binary that is actually installed — which is what the MSI's action
  does, and it now invokes it directly (FileRef) with "install" plus the instance flag: no cmd, no
  start, no wrapper script to get wrong.

  SKIPSETUP=1 IS PASSED BY THIS BUNDLE. Not to skip the setup — but because the setup would
  otherwise prompt inside a custom action while the BUNDLE is showing "Installing", which is
  two UIs at once. The bundle tells the operator what it is doing and then lets the setup open
  its own console at the end. Pass SKIPSETUP=1 yourself on the command line to install the
  binaries only.

  BUILDING: same WiX CLI as the MSI, plus the Bal extension (a separate NuGet package, and the
  same two-step install dance — `wix extension add` before `-ext` can name it):

      wix extension add -g WixToolset.Bal.wixext/5.0.2
      wix build OrchiconSetup.wxs -ext WixToolset.Bal.wixext ^
        -d Version=<ver> -d MsiPath=<msi> -d LicenseRtf=<rtf> ^
        -outputtype bundle -o OrchiconSetup.exe
-->
<Wix xmlns="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs"
     xmlns:bal="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs/bal">

  <!-- UPGRADE CODE MUST DIFFER FROM THE MSI'S.
       A bundle and a package are different products to Windows; sharing an UpgradeCode makes
       the bundle "upgrade" the MSI and removes the files out from under it. The MSI's is
       DF9438BD-B311-5C12-A1D2-EB415DF74F2D — this one is deliberately a different GUID. -->
  <Bundle Name="Orchicon"
          Version="$(var.Version)"
          Manufacturer="Orchicon"
          UpgradeCode="7C4F1D62-9A3B-4E85-B1C7-2D8E5A0F6B94"
          Copyright="Copyright 2026 beardedparrott"
          AboutUrl="https://github.com/beardedparrott/Orchicon"
          HelpUrl="https://github.com/beardedparrott/Orchicon#readme"
          UpdateUrl="https://github.com/beardedparrott/Orchicon/releases">

    <!-- THE INSTANCE, as a BUNDLE VARIABLE. It has to be declared here: `[Instance]` in an
         MsiProperty resolves against the bundle's variables, and an UNDECLARED one resolves to
         an empty string — which the MSI would refuse as an unknown instance, or (worse) accept
         as a silent default. Overridable from the command line:
             OrchiconSetup.exe Instance=prod
         defaulting to dev, exactly as the MSI and install.ps1 do. -->
    <Variable Name="Instance" Type="string" Value="dev" />

    <BootstrapperApplication>
      <!-- THE STANDARD BOOTSTRAPPER APPLICATION, with the RTF licence theme: it renders an
           RTF in a scrollable box on a licence page, which is the only theme that can carry
           the terms we actually ship. `LicenseFile` points at the SAME LICENSE.rtf the MSI's
           own licence page uses, so a person sees one set of terms whichever way they
           install.

           It also gives, with no code of ours: a progress bar per package, a cancel button, a
           "Show log" affordance, and a finish page — which is the whole reason for a bundle
           and the part an MSI cannot do for a long download. -->
      <bal:WixStandardBootstrapperApplication
          Theme="rtfLicense"
          LicenseFile="$(var.LicenseRtf)"
          SuppressOptionsUI="yes" />
    </BootstrapperApplication>

    <Chain>
      <!-- THE MSI IS THE ONLY PACKAGE. The bundle is the UI.

           THERE IS NO DisplayInternalUI ATTRIBUTE, and that is not an omission: WiX v4 REMOVED
           it. (v5 rejects it outright — "error WIX0004: The MsiPackage element contains an
           unexpected attribute 'DisplayInternalUI'".) Its own converter says the functionality
           "has fundamentally changed and requires BootstrapperApplication support": in v4+ the
           bundle's BA decides, and the standard BA does NOT display a package's internal UI. So
           a person sees one wizard, which is the point.

           SKIPSETUP=1 is passed so the setup does not try to prompt underneath the bundle's own
           progress. `Repair`/`Uninstall` are left to the bundle, which forwards to the package.

           SCOPE IS NOT SET HERE ON PURPOSE. The package is per-user (see Orchicon.wxs), and Burn
           detects a package's scope from the MSI itself — there is no PerUser attribute, and
           asking for one is a build error. -->
      <MsiPackage Id="OrchiconMsi"
                  SourceFile="$(var.MsiPath)"
                  Vital="yes"
                  Compressed="yes">
        <MsiProperty Name="INSTANCE" Value="[Instance]" />
        <MsiProperty Name="SKIPSETUP" Value="1" />
      </MsiPackage>
    </Chain>
  </Bundle>
</Wix>
